  "summary": "Distinctive religious architecture of Tibetan Buddhist monasteries and temples.",
  "description": "Characterized by massive stone structures, flat roofs, colorful murals, and distinctive golden roofs. The style reflects Buddhist cosmology, local climate adaptations, and the fusion of Indian, Nepalese, and Chinese architectural influences.",
  "culturalContext": "Tibetan monastery architecture — Potala's white-and-red mass over Lhasa, Labrang's prayer-wheel lanes, Ganden's ridge-line colleges — fused Chinese, Indian and Nepali craft into fortress-spiritual complexes: thick sloping stone walls with black trapezoidal trapezoid trapez window trims, flat roofs with timber parapets, chörtens (stupas), and prayer halls lit by butter lamps. Monasteries were city-states of learning and politics until 1959; rebuilding since preserves the trapezoid-shadow aesthetic now global through Himalayan diaspora temples.",
